WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:03.840
- This footage shows El Salvador's
answer to gang violence.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.640
Thousands of alleged gang members

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.421
stripped down to expose their tattoos

00:00:07.421 --> 00:00:12.005
in a brand new mega prison
set to house 40,000 inmates.

00:00:12.005 --> 00:00:15.922
(speaking in foreign language)

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.480
Gangs turned El Salvador's murder rate

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.280
into one of the highest in the world.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.140
And to end this cycle of violence,

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.270
the country's President, Nayib Bukele,

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:29.670
ordered a crackdown in March last year

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:33.720
arresting more than
66,000 people since then.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:36.030
Communities say the streets are safer

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:38.220
but rights groups and some residents

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:41.100
argue that this change
has come at a high price.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.410
That innocent men and women

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.360
and even minors have been rounded up

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:48.584
and held indefinitely
without access to lawyers.

00:00:48.584 --> 00:00:50.520
(speaking in foreign language)

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:54.030
I'm here in El Salvador to
gauge the impact of this effort,

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:55.950
and what it means for
families and communities,

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:57.450
and for our Central American country

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:00.690
that has been gripped by
gang violence for decades,

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:04.493
stoking both poverty and
migration towards the US.

00:01:04.493 --> 00:01:06.690
(dramatic music)

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:09.780
In El Salvador, a country
the size of Massachusetts

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:12.450
and with a population of 6.3 million,

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:15.360
the violence has been blamed on the MS 13

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.690
and the 18th Street gangs,
groups that originated

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:22.350
among Salvadorians in the US in the 1980s.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.740
They terrorized communities
for decades in El Salvador,

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:27.720
demanding weekly extortion payments

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:30.840
from business owners and imposing curfews.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.510
In 2015, El Salvador's murder rate

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:36.990
was over 100 per 100,000 people.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:41.360
But by 2022, officials said
the number went down to 7.8.

00:01:42.696 --> 00:01:46.613
(speaking in foreign language)

00:01:51.851 --> 00:01:55.230
President Nayib bukele
imposed a state of emergency

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:58.350
where lawmakers suspended
constitutional guarantees,

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:00.690
allowing soldiers to raid communities

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:03.363
in search of anyone with
suspected gang ties.

00:02:04.694 --> 00:02:06.000
(soldiers speaking in foreign language)

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.030
The mass arrests gave
El Salvador the world's

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:12.861
highest incarceration rate as
1% of the national population

00:02:12.861 --> 00:02:15.633
has been put behind
bars over the past year.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:20.040
As a result of the crackdown,

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:22.860
gang members are no longer
prevalent in communities.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.590
Small businesses can
now operate without fear

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:27.066
and at any time of the day

00:02:27.066 --> 00:02:30.003
without having to pay high extortion fees.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:34.860
But the offensive fractured
many of the poorest households.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.290
Tens of thousands of young children

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:40.260
have had at least one parent or caregiver

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:43.410
detained under the declared
state of emergency.

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:46.470
Anna Mancia says her four adult children

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:48.060
were working as street vendors

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.011
when they were detained last year.

00:02:50.011 --> 00:02:53.928
(speaking in foreign language)

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:03.390
She was left to take care
of six grandchildren.

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:07.083
None of them go to school and
Anna struggles to feed them.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:12.360
Families like Anna's can't
afford legal representation.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:15.960
Alejandro Diaz Gomez is
a nonprofit attorney.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:17.860
He advises 250 families of detainees

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:21.808
who he says are wrongfully held.

00:03:21.808 --> 00:03:25.725
(speaking in foreign language)

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:47.850
Rights groups say people are locked up

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:50.280
in crowded cells with little food

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:52.503
while they wait indefinitely for trial.

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.280
This man told us his
two sons were arrested

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.397
during the crackdown last year.

00:03:58.397 --> 00:04:03.064
(civilian speaking in foreign language)

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:09.600
El Salvador's Security
Minister Gustavo Villatoro

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.180
denied allegations of human rights abuses

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:13.920
and arbitrary detentions,

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:16.260
adding that all gang members will spend

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:18.820
the rest of their lives behind bars

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:23.220
More than a year into the crackdown.,

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:26.360
President Bukele's approval
rating is at a record high.

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:27.193
(crowd cheering)

00:04:27.193 --> 00:04:28.740
(announcer speaking in foreign language)

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:30.540
Polls at the start of the year

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:33.210
showed more than 90% of Salvadorians

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:36.180
are happy with the
President's security effort.

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:39.120
And El Salvador's model
has drawn the attention

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:41.460
of other Latin American countries gripped

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:45.840
by violent crime such
as Ecuador and Honduras.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:49.140
Political analysts say,
the crackdown's popularity

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:53.250
could pave the way for bukele
to seek reelection next year.

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:55.050
While the country's constitution

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:56.730
does not allow a president

00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:58.920
to have consecutive terms in office,

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:00.854
he has said he plans to run again.

00:05:00.854 --> 00:05:02.420
(crowd cheering)

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:05.003
(gentle music)

